"helpText" = "## How to Use\n1. Hold the Fn (globe) key and press J, K, L or another key.\n2. The app gets the same input as from the keypad key. You can type numbers with the right hand alone on a keyboard without a keypad.\n- ⇧, ⌃, ⌥ and ⌘ pressed together are sent along (for example, Fn+⇧+J is ⇧+keypad 1).\n- Holding a key repeats the converted key.\n\n## Trigger Key\n- Under Keys → Trigger Key in Settings, choose Fn, Caps Lock or Both for US and other keyboards and for JIS keyboards. At first, US and others use Fn and JIS uses Caps Lock.\n- The keyboard type is checked for each key you press. The keyboard in the main window shows the chosen trigger keys in blue.\n- After Caps Lock was used, the caps lock goes back to how it was before the press. Pressing Caps Lock alone toggles it as usual.\n- If Karabiner-Elements or another tool turns Caps Lock into another key, it cannot be used as Caps Lock.\n\n## Key Assignments\n- **Keypad layout**: 7 8 9 → 7 8 9, U I O → 4 5 6, J K L → 1 2 3, M → 0, N → 00\n- **Number row**: 1–6 → keypad 1–6, 0 and ` → keypad 0\n- **Operators and decimal point**: P → *, ; → +, ' and - → −, / → /, . → ., = → =, , → ,, Tab → =\n- **Tab, Delete, Return**: [ → Tab, Y → Delete, H → Return, Return → Enter (keypad)\n- Keys are named by their place on the US layout. On a JIS keyboard, the key right of ; (:) is ' and the key right of P (@) is [.\n\n## Main Window\n- Shows the conversion status and the key table. Groups not in use are dimmed.\n- Enable keypad conversion turns all conversion on or off.\n\n## Settings\n- Startup & Background: open at login (off at first), hide main window at startup, hide from Dock and App Switcher, keep running when the window is closed, the menu bar icon, Accessibility status, and the relaunch and quit buttons.\n- Keys: keypad conversion on or off, and each of the four groups.\n- Excluded Apps: apps where nothing is converted; keys pressed with Fn are passed on unchanged.\n- Open Settings with ⌘, and close it with ⌘W.\n\n## Permissions\n- **Accessibility**: needed to receive the keys pressed with Fn and replace them with keypad keys. Turn on FnNumpad in System Settings → Privacy & Security → Accessibility. Conversion starts without relaunching.\n\n## Staying Open and Quitting\n- FnNumpad stays in the menu bar. Conversion continues after the window is closed.\n- Show the main window again from the menu bar icon, the Dock icon, by opening the app again in the Finder, or with ⌘0.\n- With Hide from Dock and App Switcher on, the icon is not shown in the Dock or the ⌘Tab switcher. Show the window from the menu bar icon or by opening the app again in the Finder.\n- To stop for a while, choose Pause (until relaunch) from the menu bar icon.\n- Quit with Quit FnNumpad in the menu bar icon, or ⌘Q while the app is in front.\n\n## Karabiner-Elements\n- The assignments are the same as the Karabiner-Elements rule Fn+Number to KeyPad, Fn+[ / Y / H to Tab / Delete / Return. Turn that rule off in Karabiner-Elements when you use FnNumpad. Keys are not converted twice with both on, but you cannot tell which one is working.\n\n## The Comma of Fn+,\n- Japanese input turns the comma key into 「、」, so while an input method is on, \",\" is pasted through the clipboard. The clipboard is put back after about 0.6 seconds (anything newly copied in the meantime is kept). With a plain layout, the comma key is sent.\n- The pasted \",\" is marked as transient (nspasteboard.org TransientType), so clipboard history apps that support it do not keep it.\n\n## Limitations\n- Keys are not converted where macOS protects the input (secure input, such as password fields).\n- If System Settings → Keyboard assigns a macOS feature to the globe (Fn) key with a key (such as Fn+E for emoji), that feature may come first.\n- External keyboards without an Fn key cannot be used.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- **Nothing is converted**: check the Accessibility permission, Enable keypad conversion, that it is not paused, that the front app is not excluded, and that the key's group is in use. The Status in the main window gives the reason.\n- **Allowed but still not converted**: turn FnNumpad off and on again in the Accessibility list in System Settings, then relaunch FnNumpad.\n- **No numbers in some app**: some apps handle keypad keys their own way. Add the app to Excluded Apps to pass the original keys on.\n\n## Sharing Settings\n- Under Export & Import on the Sharing tab, you can export settings to a file (FnNumpad-Settings.plist) and import them, for moving to another Mac or keeping a copy. Importing asks for confirmation first.\n- Under Settings Sync, choose a folder such as iCloud Drive or Dropbox to sync settings between Macs that use the same folder.\n- Shared: whether keypad conversion is on and which key groups are used. Launch at login, staying open, the menu bar icon and excluded apps stay on each Mac.\n\n## Privacy\n- Everything happens on this Mac; nothing is sent out (except checking for updates).\n- The keys you press are not saved in files or logs.";
